@Mark - I got hung up on LEyDEN, too. Until the crosses showed me that the alternative spelling was needed. 

INDUCER is, indeed, a valid genetic/biochemical term. I think the first well known example is that allolactose is the INDUCER of the lac operon. It INDUCEs the transcription of the lac operon by interfering with the binding of the repressor molecule (repressor being, more or less, the opposite of an INDUCER.) 

INDUCERS can also act by binding to activators and boosting their efficacy. An example is maltose metabolism in bacteria, where the operon that produces the necessary enzymes to metabolize lactose is turned on in the presence of maltose, which binds to an activator protein which then intimates transcription. In this case, maltose is the INDUCER.

